.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_QUERY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_query \- look up all values for a query parameter
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

const char **chttpsvr_req_query(chttpsvr_req *req, const char *key,
                                 size_t *count_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Parses the query string lazily on the first call and returns a
NULL-terminated array of all the URL-decoded values of key.  Each key
of the query string is URL-decoded and then compared with key as you
give it, in the same way as url.Values of the Go net/url package, so
pass the decoded form: "a b" finds both a+b and a%20b, but "a%20b"
finds neither.  When count_out is not NULL the count goes into it.
For an absent key the function returns NULL.

A key=value pair whose key or value has a malformed percent-escape,
such as a=%zz, or an escape that decodes to a NUL byte, such as
a=%00, is left out as if the query string did not contain it.  Read
chttpsvr_req_raw_query(3) if you need to see such a pair.

The value strings that the array points to are valid until the
handler call returns.  The array itself is a scratch buffer of this
request that every chttpsvr_req_query call reuses, so copy the
pointers you need before you call chttpsvr_req_query again on the
same request.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function can't tell a lack of memory, while the query string is
parsed or while the result array grows, from a key that is really
absent: both give NULL and a count of 0.  After a NULL, call
chttpsvr_req_query_oom(req) to find out which one it was, or use
chttpsvr_req_query_one for a key with one value, since that function
returns ccol_not_enough_memory when it runs out of memory.

A component of the query string that has a "=" with an empty key in
front of it, for example "?=hello", is a real pair that you can look
up: the key "" maps to the value "hello".  A completely empty
component is different, and the parser skips it instead of reading it
as an entry with an empty key and an empty value.  A component is
completely empty when there is nothing at all between two "&"
characters, before a leading "&" or after a trailing "&", as in
"?a=1&&b=2" and "?&a=1".
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_req_query_one (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_query_oom (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_raw_query (3)
